## 3. Transformer: The Breakthrough

The Transformer was introduced in the 2017 paper "Attention Is All You Need." Instead of reading words one-by-one like an RNN, the transformer looks at **all words simultaneously** and asks: "For each word, which other words should I pay attention to?"

### 3.1 The Core Components: Three Weight Matrices

The transformer has **three permanent weight matrices** (per attention layer), learned during training and saved as part of the model file. These are the "filter machines" that transform each token into three specialised versions:

| Matrix | Name | Analogy | Purpose |
|--------|------|---------|---------|
| **W_Q** | Query | Your wishlist | "What am I looking for?" — highlights the aspects of a token that help it search for relevant partners |
| **W_K** | Key | Your name badge | "What do I offer?" — highlights the aspects that help OTHER tokens decide if this token is relevant to them |
| **W_V** | Value | Your briefcase | "What's my actual meaning?" — carries the real semantic content that gets shared when another token pays attention |

> 💡 **Why three matrices, not one?** A single representation of "cats" can't encode "I need verbs" (Query), "I am a noun" (Key), and "I mean feline" (Value) all at once with equal clarity. Three specialised versions solve this.

### 3.2 Where Do These Matrices Come From?

```
Day 0 (Before training)     →  W_Q filled with random numbers (noise)
                                Model produces garbage predictions

Training (weeks on GPUs)    →  Model reads billions of sentences, tries to
                                predict next word, gets it wrong, adjusts
                                W_Q/W_K/W_V numbers slightly via gradient
                                descent. Repeated billions of times.

After training              →  W_Q/W_K/W_V contain useful values.
                                The model file IS these matrices.
```

When people say "Llama 3 is a 70B model and the download is 140GB" — that 140GB file is mostly these matrices. **No human chose these numbers.** The training algorithm found them automatically through trial and error at massive scale.

### 3.3 How the Multiplication Works

The multiplication is a **filter** — you feed in a token's embedding vector, the matrix amplifies some aspects and dampens others, and out comes a purpose-built version.

```
Same W_Q machine, different inputs, different outputs:

"cats"  → [W_Q machine] → Q_cats  = "I need verbs about duration/lifespan"
"How"   → [W_Q machine] → Q_How   = "I need words like long/much/many"
"live?" → [W_Q machine] → Q_live? = "I need the subject — who lives?"
```

The W_Q matrix is the **same for every token** — what makes each output different is the token itself. Same machine, different inputs, different results.

